<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Archbtw on 0x32++</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/tags/archbtw/</link><description>Recent content in Archbtw on 0x32++</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Tue, 22 Sep 2026 08:00:10 +0100</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://blog.0x32.co.uk/tags/archbtw/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Arch + Hyprland: Results So Far</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/arch-hyprland-results-so-far/</link><pubDate>Tue, 22 Sep 2026 08:00:10 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/arch-hyprland-results-so-far/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;Follow-up to &lt;a href="https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/building-it-the-boring-way-arch-hyprland-from-scratch/"&gt;Building It the Boring Way: Arch + Hyprland From Scratch&lt;/a&gt; — turned out easier than I thought, once I got past one annoying snag right at the start.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="wp-caption alignnone"&gt;&#10; &lt;a data-flickr-embed="true" data-header="true" data-footer="false"&#10; data-context="false"&#10; href="https://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/55542039423"&#10; title="ArchBTW desktop"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://live.staticflickr.com/65535/55542039423_2b7624e059_z.jpg" width="800" alt="ArchBTW desktop" /&gt;&#10; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;script async src="//embedr.flickr.com/assets/client-code.js" charset="utf-8"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption class="wp-caption-text"&gt;Arch &amp;#43; Hyprland up and running on the old laptop&lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10; &lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;p&gt;The only real hiccup was WiFi from the Arch install ISO — &lt;code&gt;iwctl&lt;/code&gt; didn&amp;rsquo;t want to cooperate at first, so getting the installer online took longer than every other step combined. Once that was sorted, the rest of the plan went pretty much exactly as laid out: &lt;code&gt;archinstall&lt;/code&gt; for the base system, then Hyprland and the usual Wayland bits on top.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>